notes.ai — running assistant notes (user-managed)
====================================================

ASSISTANT REMINDER
------------------
This file exists to preserve continuity across chats. Append concise project decisions, key requirements,
and “latest artifact” pointers here whenever meaningful progress is made, so the user can re-upload/paste
notes.ai in a new chat and we can resume without losing context.

How to use
----------
- This file is meant to be kept in your repo (project root) and re-uploaded/pasted into a new chat
  if you want continuity.
- I will append concise, factual notes here when we make decisions/changes, so we can recover context
  if the chat truncates.
- Format: dated entries + actionable details (no hidden reasoning).

Project
-------
- Tetris NeoPixel matrix firmware (PlatformIO + Arduino on ESP32-S3 DevKitC-1).
- Key user requirement: brightness behavior differs by power source.

Key requirement (brightness)
----------------------------
- Desired:
  - USB charger / adapter: BRIGHTNESS = 95
  - PC USB port: BRIGHTNESS = 15 (to reduce current draw / glare)
- Observation:
  - Enumeration-only detection was unreliable in user's setup (still went bright).
  - Serial-port-open trigger behaved acceptably: when programming/monitoring begins, it dims.

Latest approach (as of 2026-01-20)
-------------------------------
- Use USB-CDC Serial open as the "PC present" signal.
  - Default brightness: 95.
  - If Serial becomes true (CDC opened by PlatformIO monitor/upload tooling), set brightness to 15.
  - Optional latch: keep 15 until reboot (default ON in v8).
- Rationale:
  - Avoid MCU/USB-stack specific hooks (TinyUSB mount, USB.h) per user request.
  - Aligns with observed workflow: starting programming causes dimming.

Artifacts
---------
- Latest zip produced in chat: "tetris-main-serial-dim-v8.zip" (serial-trigger brightness).

Open questions / next checks
----------------------------
- If user wants "always dim on PC even without monitor/programming", serial-open trigger won't catch it.
  Options would require:
  - USB enumeration/config detection (MCU/stack-specific), or
  - hardware power sensing (VBUS current, negotiation, etc.), or
  - user-controlled mode (button/setting).

